messages.content:
{
  "acknowledgement": "Thanks for the update. Data is solid now (5G, fast web), so the remaining blockers are typically settings around Wi‑Fi Calling/Data Saver/VPN or the Messages app’s permissions/background data. We’ll verify and fix those next.",
  "thought_process": "MMS rides on mobile data but uses special routing via the MMSC. With APN reset + reboot done and data performing well, common on-device causes are: Wi‑Fi Calling conflicts, Data Saver/VPN blocking MMS, or the Messages app missing Storage/SMS permissions or restricted background data. We’ll rule these out one by one and then retest.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Confirm Wi‑Fi Calling is OFF",
      "how": "Settings > Network/Connections > Wi‑Fi Calling > set to Off.",
      "why": "Some devices/carriers don’t pass MMS reliably with Wi‑Fi Calling enabled."
    },
    {
      "title": "Ensure Data Saver is OFF and no VPN is active",
      "how": "Data Saver: Settings > Network/Connections > Data Saver > Off. VPN: Settings > VPN > ensure no active connection.",
      "why": "Either can block or throttle MMS traffic even when general browsing works."
    },
    {
      "title": "Check Messages app permissions",
      "how": "Settings > Apps > Messages > Permissions. Allow: Photos and videos (or Storage) and SMS (Send messages).",
      "why": "MMS needs access to media files and SMS/MMS functions."
    },
    {
      "title": "Allow background data for Messages",
      "how": "Settings > Apps > Messages > Mobile data or Data usage > enable Allow background data usage and Allow data usage while Data Saver is on (if available).",
      "why": "Prevents the system from blocking MMS transfers in the background."
    },
    {
      "title": "Retest MMS",
      "how": "Keep Wi‑Fi OFF. Send a small photo to your own number. If it still fails, also try sending the same photo to a different contact.",
      "why": "Confirms whether MMS now passes and rules out a loopback quirk when sending to self."
    }
  ],
  "advanced_checks_if_still_failing": [
    {
      "title": "Quick MMS capability check",
      "how": "Run an MMS diagnostic on the phone (can_send_mms) if available, or in Messages try attaching a very small image (<200 KB) to reduce size as a variable.",
      "why": "Validates the app’s ability to initiate MMS and removes size as a factor."
    },
    {
      "title": "Verify APN has an MMSC set",
      "how": "Settings > Mobile network/Connections > Access Point Names > open the active APN and confirm the MMSC field is populated (don’t change values; just verify).",
      "why": "Even after a reset, a missing MMSC would prevent MMS."
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    "After turning Wi‑Fi Calling OFF, confirming Data Saver/VPN are OFF, and granting the Messages app Storage/Photos and SMS permissions (with background data allowed), does the MMS to yourself send?",
    "If not, what exact behavior do you see—does it still say “Message not sent” immediately or after a delay?",
    "Could you also try sending that same small photo to a different contact and tell me what happens?",
    "Which Android brand/model is it (e.g., Samsung Galaxy, Google Pixel)? I can tailor any menu names if needed."
  ],
  "next": "Please complete Steps 1–5 and share the results. If it still fails, we’ll use the advanced checks to pinpoint whether it’s an app/MMSC path issue and proceed from there."
}

